+# M4 implementation plan — remaining runtime behavior and capacity qualification
+
+Planning pass: sol, medium reasoning. Implementation/qualification pass: luna,
+xhigh reasoning. This plan follows, in precedence order,
+`docs/macos-m3-handoff.md`, `docs/macos-m3-result.md`,
+`docs/macos-m1-handoff.md`, and the immutable M4 section of
+`docs/macos-arm64-plan.md`.
+
+## Outcome and governing rule
+
+M4 is an evidence-first hardware qualification slice. It is complete only when
+all eight scope items have a real Apple Silicon disposition and the result
+record contains measured numbers. A test that compiles, a unit test, an
+inspection of an earlier commit, and a control response containing `ok:true`
+are not hardware acceptance.
+
+The normal implementation change is a new M4 test/evidence harness, fixtures,
+and `docs/macos-m4-result.md`. Do not change product/runtime code in advance.
+If hardware exposes a defect, preserve the failing evidence, identify the
+smallest owning product/runtime file, make the narrow fix, add a regression
+test, rebuild, and rerun the failed hardware item plus any affected earlier
+item. Do not use M4 to redesign the profile model, port contract, sync engine,
+browser-equivalent HTTP/WS pattern, guest provisioning, or launcher lifecycle.
+
+## Preflight and evidence contract
+
+Before consuming Mac state, luna must:
+
+1. Work from a fresh branch/worktree off the then-current
+   `luna/macos-m3-ux`, unless the owner explicitly chooses otherwise. Run and
+   record `git log --oneline -5` and `git status` before doing any work because
+   this branch has had concurrent writers. Never use `git add -A` or
+   `git add .`; stage only the exact owned paths. Reconcile commit `9916fb9`
+   and any newer concurrent changes before building.
+2. Use only `iolbox-m4-e2e` (new) or another owner-approved disposable VM.
+   Refuse `--machine iol22` in the harness. `iol22` is irreplaceable M0
+   evidence and must never be started, stopped, modified, or deleted.
+3. Record Mac facts (`sw_vers`, `uname -a`, `sysctl -n hw.memsize`,
+   `sysctl -n hw.ncpu`), `/opt/homebrew/bin/limactl --version`,
+   `/opt/homebrew/bin/limactl list`, available disk, and the exact source
+   revision and SHA-256 hashes of the Darwin launcher/test binaries, IOL image,
+   fixtures, and packaging asset tree used.
+4. Record actual reclaimable memory before creating or starting a VM with
+   `top -l 1 -s 0 | grep PhysMem`. Also record `memory_pressure -Q`,
+   `sysctl vm.swapusage`, and `vm_stat`, but do not use raw `vm_stat` free pages
+   as the RAM gate. Homebrew/Lima are not on the non-login path; use
+   `/opt/homebrew/bin/limactl` explicitly. All Mac shell must run on Bash
+   3.2.57.
+5. Build the Darwin arm64 launcher and opt-in Go test binary off-host in the
+   established manner, stage them to the Mac, and run the ordinary launcher Go
+   tests, `go vet`, Darwin arm64 build/test compile, Windows amd64 build, and
+   existing Windows tests. Record these separately as compile/unit evidence,
+   never as hardware evidence.
+6. Create one timestamped evidence root such as
+   `~/iolbox-m1/evidence-m4/m4-<UTC>-<pid>/`. Every command gets its own stdout,
+   stderr, exit-status, start UTC, and end UTC record. Put a machine-readable
+   `summary.json`, minute samples in CSV/NDJSON, raw console transcripts,
+   control responses, pcapng files, and a human-readable `README.txt` in it.
+   The harness must trap normal interruption and record `INCOMPLETE`; it must
+   not convert an interrupted 119-minute soak into a pass.
+7. Start from a stopped, clean disposable machine and establish a numeric idle
+   baseline. Snapshot guest links, bridges, taps, routes, namespaces, iptables,
+   listeners, cgroups, and relevant processes before loading a lab. A later
+   cleanup pass compares against this baseline rather than assuming that an
+   empty-looking `ps` excerpt is enough.
+
+### Common numeric sampler
+
+Use one recorder throughout every runtime item, at minimum at `before`,
+`after lab.load`, `after lab.start`, `ready`, and `after lab.stop`. During the
+soak sample every 60 seconds using UTC timestamps and elapsed monotonic seconds.
+Record raw source output as well as parsed columns.
+
+| Required measurement | Concrete source and fields |
+|---|---|
+| Boot time | Monotonic duration in milliseconds from accepted `lab.start` request to the last required node's real usable console prompt. Also retain each node's individual duration, `lab.start` started/failed arrays, and first-prompt text. VM/GUI readiness is separately timed from launcher invocation to `GET /` returning a status below 500. |
+| Packet loss | For every traffic direction, retain the last complete IOS/VPCS success summary and parse transmitted, received, lost, and loss percentage. Record cold/warm tests separately. Soak CSV contains those four fields for each interval plus cumulative totals; never infer loss from capture packet count. |
+| Load | Guest `/proc/loadavg` (1/5/15 minute fields and runnable/total tasks) plus `uptime`; host `top -l 1 -s 0` load averages. |
+| Per-node RSS | Discover node PIDs from the supervisor's returned node/process identity where available and corroborate with full command lines. For each actual IOL/VPCS child record PID, node id/name, RSS KiB from `/proc/<pid>/status` (`VmRSS`) and `ps -o pid,ppid,rss,vsz,etime,command`. Do not aggregate four nodes into one number. |
+| Guest memory | `free -b`, selected `/proc/meminfo` fields (`MemTotal`, `MemAvailable`, `MemFree`, `Buffers`, `Cached`, `SwapTotal`, `SwapFree`), and guest OOM/kernel evidence (`journalctl -k` since run start). Record bytes and compute used/available percentage in the summary. |
+| Host memory pressure | Raw `top -l 1 -s 0` PhysMem and load lines, `memory_pressure -Q`, `sysctl vm.swapusage`, and the Lima/VZ process RSS from `ps`. Parse wired, active, compressed, unused, swap used, and the tool's free/pressure percentage where present. The minimum, maximum, and final values belong in `summary.json`. |
+| Capture health | Bytes, complete blocks, SHB/IDB count, enhanced/simple packet count, first/last packet timestamp when present, SHA-256, collector connection status, and the elapsed time of the last received packet. Store validation results at the start, every 30 minutes, and the end. |
+
+The record must include the exact numeric value even when a bar only says
+“zero” or “non-zero.” Missing, unparsable, or stale samples are failures of the
+qualification harness, not values to report as zero.
+
+## Hardware execution order
+
+The intended timeline is:
+
+1. Preflight, clean baseline, and harness self-check.
+2. Item 1, VPCS-to-IOL end to end, by itself and first.
+3. Item 2, multi-link topology plus live-capture start; once it passes its
+   initial traffic check, immediately convert the same stable topology into
+   item 6's two-hour soak.
+4. While the soak collector and traffic generator continue in the background,
+   perform item 3's NAT start/traffic/stop on a separate link/node in the same
+   loaded fixture, then item 4's extnet capability/interface probe and, if
+   possible without disrupting the soak, its attach/traffic/cleanup test.
+   Continue monitoring the soak after those short items finish. Do not restart
+   the supervisor, launcher, or VM, change the port contract, or run another
+   harness that restarts the service while the soak is active.
+5. When the full 120 minutes and final capture validation complete, stop that
+   lab cleanly and preserve its evidence. Only then release its RAM and run
+   item 5, four IOL nodes.
+6. Stop the four-node lab, return to a normal multi-kind lab, and run item 7's
+   two forced-termination recovery cases last. This is destructive to running
+   test state and therefore must not precede or overlap the soak.
+7. Take the final item 8 sample/summary, run cleanup checks, stop (do not
+   delete) the M4 guest, and write the result document from the recorded
+   evidence.
+
+This ordering starts the expensive clock after the mandatory VPCS proof and
+only one topology setup. It also keeps the four-node peak and forced VM stop
+out of the soak's measurement window.
+
+## Item-by-item execution and bars
+
+### 1. VPCS connected to IOL with bidirectional ping
+
+This is the first real hardware step. Commit `9916fb9` is context, not proof.
+
+Run a minimal two-node fixture: one IOL L3 node at explicit `ram: 1024` and one
+VPCS node on a p2p link. Give the IOL and VPCS static addresses in one /24.
+Load via the browser-equivalent `/control` route, inspect `warnings`, start both
+nodes, wait for both real prompts, and capture full consoles. Send 10 cold
+pings in each direction to expose startup/ARP behavior, then 100 warm pings in
+each direction. For IOS, enter privileged EXEC before using extended ping
+keywords. For VPCS, use its real console syntax and match its final summary,
+not echoed input.
+
+Record node start results, individual prompt/boot times, exact transmitted and
+received counts, loss percentage and latency summary for all four samples,
+per-node RSS, guest/host memory, and load. Snapshot the `vpcs` process, its
+console listener, link devices, and node state while running and after stop.
+
+Pass bar: both node prompts are usable; neither node appears in `failed`; each
+warm direction sends exactly 100 and receives at least 99 (at most 1% loss);
+no unexplained loss may be hidden by rerunning; and stop removes the VPCS/IOL
+children and link artifacts. The cold results are recorded even if the known
+first-packet ARP loss occurs. Failure to start the PC node is a real M4 failure
+to diagnose/fix, not a skip based on the commit.
+
+### 2. Multi-link topology with capture
+
+Use an M4 combined fixture with two 1024 MB IOL routers, one VPCS, and one NAT
+node. Its independent links are R1–R2, VPCS–R1, and R2–NAT; thus it exercises
+more than the prior two-node p2p case and both IOL fabric interfaces. Start the
+two IOL nodes and VPCS first; NAT remains stopped until item 3. Configure
+addresses/routes in explicit startup configurations. Open all three consoles
+where applicable, disable IOS pagination, and prove traffic on both active
+links (VPCS↔R1 and R1↔R2). Start live capture on the R1–R2 link before the
+traffic and keep the authenticated `/capture/{link}` WebSocket open.
+
+Record the loaded lab JSON and returned lab id/node/console mapping, every
+`lab.start` result, prompt times, per-link bidirectional 100-packet warm ping
+counts, device/bridge membership, and a capture checkpoint with bytes, packet
+count, first/last packet time, and SHA-256.
+
+Pass bar: all three started nodes reach real prompts; both links carry traffic
+in both directions with at most 1% warm loss per direction; link/device mapping
+matches the fixture; the capture is structurally valid pcapng with SHB, IDB,
+at least one packet block, symmetric block lengths, and packets observed after
+the test traffic begins; capture remains connected. A corrupt fabric or
+capture is release-blocking.
+
+### 3. NAT-node outbound connectivity and teardown
+
+While item 6 continues over R1–R2, start the NAT node and its R2–NAT link.
+First require `hello.features` to advertise `natgw`; capture the actual default
+route/interface. Configure the connected IOS interface by DHCP (or the
+fixture's documented static address within the allocated 172.31.n.0/24 if IOS
+DHCP proves unavailable), record the lease/address/default route, ping the NAT
+gateway, then ping a stable numeric address reachable through the Mac/VM.
+Resolve and record the chosen address before the run; use numeric traffic for
+the acceptance bar so DNS is not confused with NAT. Run at least 20 warm
+outbound packets. Record `ip addr`, `ip route`, `ip link`, `iptables-save`, NAT
+tap/bridge membership, and packet counters before traffic, after traffic, and
+after `node.stop`/link teardown.
+
+Pass bar: NAT start is not in `failed`; the client obtains/uses an address in
+the advertised subnet; gateway ping succeeds; at least 19 of 20 numeric
+outbound pings return; the matching MASQUERADE/FORWARD packet counters increase;
+and teardown leaves zero matching `iolnat<id>` device, zero fixture bridge/link
+attachment, zero M4 subnet iptables rules, and no NAT endpoint state. The soak
+traffic/capture must continue across NAT start and teardown with no broken WS
+connection or failed interval.
+
+### 4. extnet attach, traffic, and cleanup when exercisable
+
+Do not silently equate NAT with extnet. Record a capability report containing
+`hello.features`, the current accepted lab node kinds, Lima VM type/network
+configuration (`limactl list --json` or the 2.2.0 equivalent), guest
+`ip -details link`, `ip addr`, `ip route`, and the default-route interface.
+An exercisable host needs both a product-supported external/management attach
+capability and a suitable independent non-loopback Ethernet-like guest
+interface that is not merely the VM default-route/NAT interface. Record the
+candidate interface name, type, state, addresses, and why it is safe to use.
+
+If those prerequisites exist, load the smallest fixture supported by the
+current contract (one IOL plus the extnet/management endpoint), attach it to the
+named interface, record before/attached/after link state, exchange at least 20
+packets in both directions with a known peer on that L2 network, and stop it.
+Pass requires at least 19/20 in each direction and zero residual macvtap/tap,
+bridge membership, routes, or processes/rules owned by that fixture.
+
+If Lima exposes no such interface, or the current accepted node contract has no
+external/management attach kind, item 4's honest disposition is
+`NOT EXERCISABLE ON THIS HOST`, not PASS and not silent SKIP. The result must
+include the raw capability/interface evidence and the precise failed
+prerequisite. Do not invent a product feature or repurpose the default NAT
+interface merely to manufacture a pass. This documented limitation is allowed
+by the immutable plan; observed corruption or leaks in an available path are
+not.
+
+### 5. Four IOL nodes on supported hardware
+
+Run only after the soak lab is stopped. Use a four-IOL ring fixture with four
+explicit `ram: 1024` L3 nodes and four independent p2p links (R1–R2–R3–R4–R1),
+unique /30s, and static routes or a minimal routing configuration that permits
+an end-to-end R1↔R3 test. Start all four in one `lab.start`, open all four
+consoles concurrently, and wait for each real prompt. Run a 100-packet warm
+end-to-end ping in both directions and a one-hop check over every link. Take
+resource samples at idle, start acceptance, every 15 seconds during boot,
+ready, traffic, and after stop.
+
+Record each node's boot time and RSS, aggregate node RSS, guest memory available
+and swap, host pressure/compression/swap and Lima/VZ RSS, load averages, all
+ping counts, and any OOM/MALLOCFAIL/kernel or supervisor messages.
+
+Pass bar: all four nodes start in the same 4 GiB Debian 13 profile guest, reach
+usable prompts within the existing 150-second per-node readiness budget, and
+remain alive through traffic; every adjacent link passes warm traffic and both
+end-to-end directions receive at least 99/100; there is no OOM kill,
+`%SYS-2-MALLOCFAIL`, persistent swap thrash that prevents readiness, or missing
+sample; stop removes all four processes and fabric devices. Do not reduce node
+count, lower RAM below 1024 MB, substitute VPCS, or call sequential two-node
+runs equivalent.
+
+If the host cannot meet that bar after the RAM-headroom procedure below, record
+the exact failure point and final resource numbers and ask the owner whether a
+16 GB Apple Silicon Mac is required. The acceptance state remains BLOCKED/
+UNVERIFIED, not PASS.
+
+### 6. Two-hour sustained traffic soak with capture throughout
+
+After item 2's initial pass, keep the combined lab and its R1–R2 capture alive.
+The M4 Go test must launch a background traffic loop and capture collector that
+survive while the foreground NAT/extnet checks run. Use fixed-duration traffic
+intervals (for example, a 30- or 60-packet privileged IOS ping with one-second
+timeout each minute) so each interval produces a separately parseable summary.
+Continue for at least 7,200 monotonic seconds; wall-clock timestamps are for
+audit, not the duration calculation. Send traffic every minute with no planned
+gap longer than 90 seconds.
+
+Keep one authenticated same-origin capture WS open for the full run. Append
+binary frames to one pcapng file. At elapsed 0, 30, 60, 90, and 120 minutes,
+validate a complete-block snapshot without truncating the live file, record
+bytes/blocks/packets/SHA-256 and the last packet timestamp, and inject a unique
+traffic checkpoint so “still valid” cannot be satisfied by replaying the first
+minute. The collector must continue draining while foreground tests run; a
+full socket buffer is not a soak.
+
+Record one metrics row per minute plus every traffic interval. Pass bar: elapsed
+monotonic time is at least 7,200 seconds; at least 120 traffic summaries and 121
+resource samples (including endpoints) are present; cumulative loss is no more
+than 1%, no individual interval has unexplained total failure; capture remains
+one live connection, every checkpoint is structurally valid, packet count and
+last-packet time advance at every checkpoint, and final capture has packets
+from the last five minutes; all required nodes stay at usable prompts with no
+unexpected restart, OOM, or wedged console. NAT/extnet foreground work must not
+break the soak. Any collector reconnect, invalid block, capture stall, process
+death, or shortened duration fails this item and requires a fresh full two-hour
+rerun after the cause is fixed.
+
+### 7. Forced launcher and VM termination, then clean restart
+
+Run two explicit recovery cases, preserving before/after snapshots.
+
+1. **Forced launcher termination:** from a stopped M4 VM, start the Darwin
+   launcher in the background and send SIGKILL to that exact PID during an
+   observed in-progress lifecycle step (record the phase and timestamps; never
+   use a broad `pkill`). Rerun launcher `start`, wait for real HTTP readiness,
+   load/start the recovery fixture, then stop it normally.
+2. **Forced VM termination:** start the multi-kind recovery fixture including
+   VPCS, IOL, NAT, fabric, and live capture. Snapshot artifacts, then use the
+   exact Lima 2.2.0 forced-stop command confirmed by `limactl stop --help`
+   against only the disposable M4 machine. Restart through the launcher and
+   wait for `GET / < 500`; do not treat Lima `Running` as ready.
+
+After each restart, before starting a new lab, record and assert: zero old IOL
+image or `vpcs` child PIDs; zero old node console/capture listeners; no M4
+`iol<instance>_<port>`, `iolbr<link>`, or `iolnat<node>` devices; no M4 NAT
+iptables rules/subnet; no stale tool netns/veth/cgroup record if a PC/tool was
+part of the fixture; and no stale capture collector. Compare PIDs and objects
+to the pre-kill snapshot so newly created valid supervisor infrastructure is
+not misclassified. Then start and stop a small lab once to prove recovery is
+functional, not merely clean-looking.
+
+Pass bar: both kill actions are proven by non-zero/forced termination status;
+both subsequent launcher starts reach real readiness; every old artifact count
+above is exactly zero before the recovery lab starts; the recovery lab passes
+bidirectional traffic and clean stop; guest/host data remain present. Any stale
+tap/process/rule or need to delete data/VM to recover is release-blocking.
+
+### 8. Final numeric qualification record
+
+Item 8 is not a prose exercise. Generate `summary.json` from raw evidence and
+copy its actual values into `docs/macos-m4-result.md`: host/profile/product/build
+identity; per-item verdict; VM and node boot milliseconds; ping sent/received/
+loss and latency summaries; guest/host load minima/maxima; every node's peak
+RSS; guest memory minimum available and swap maximum; host pressure,
+compression, unused and swap extrema; capture duration/bytes/packet count and
+checkpoint hashes; soak elapsed time/sample count; teardown object counts; and
+the RAM-headroom actions actually taken.
+
+Pass bar: every required metric has a raw source and a parsed real number, all
+seven behavioral items have PASS or the explicitly permitted item-4
+`NOT EXERCISABLE` disposition with evidence, hardware work is distinguished
+from compile/unit work, and every failure/unverified criterion is reported
+honestly. Four-node inability is not an allowed PASS disposition.
+
+## RAM-headroom procedure for the 8 GB Mac
+
+Do not delete anything merely because raw `vm_stat` looks low. Before item 5:
+
+1. Stop the M4 soak lab cleanly and confirm its IOL/VPCS/NAT processes are gone.
+   Stop any other *running* disposable VM, one at a time, and after each action
+   record `limactl list`, `top -l 1 -s 0 | grep PhysMem`, `memory_pressure -Q`,
+   and swap. Never touch `iol22`.
+2. Close only known nonessential processes owned by the qualification session
+   (stale test binaries/capture probes) using exact PIDs. Do not kill arbitrary
+   user applications.
+3. If another disposable VM is still running, stop in this order:
+   `m1jammy`, `m1trixie`, `iolbox-m1-e2e`, `iolbox-m2-e2e`, then
+   `iolbox-m3-e2e`. The canary VMs are cheapest to recreate; M3's stopped guest
+   with the final passing state is preserved longest.
+4. Deleting a stopped VM frees disk, not live RAM. Delete only if Lima/VZ still
+   retains resources or disk pressure prevents the M4 run, and use the same
+   order: `m1jammy`, `m1trixie`, `iolbox-m1-e2e`, `iolbox-m2-e2e`,
+   `iolbox-m3-e2e`. Before each delete, resolve the exact machine name with
+   `limactl list`, record it, and recheck that it is not `iol22` and not the
+   active M4 machine. Record which machines were stopped versus deleted.
+5. Retry item 5 once from a cold stopped M4 guest after headroom is reclaimed.
+   Keep the required four nodes and 1024 MB each unchanged. If the same hard
+   wall recurs, stop. Preserve OOM/start/resource evidence and escalate to the
+   owner for the unmade 8 GB-versus-16 GB acceptance decision. Do not silently
+   resize scope, overcommit beyond the supported profile, or declare success
+   on a larger/different configuration without owner direction.
+
+## Harness reuse and new test/record work
+
+### Reuse without redesign
+
+- Reuse the M3 host orchestration pattern in
+  `packaging/macos/tests/hardware-m3.sh`: timestamped evidence directory,
+  explicit `/opt/homebrew/bin/limactl`, protected machine assertion, Mac
+  resource gate, staged Darwin launcher/test binary, and distinct compile/
+  hardware records.
+- Reuse the M1 collector's correlated control and evidence discipline, its
